After /cd, the new directory's [directories] allow list now narrows the file tools. Before this change it was ignored. It's committed as 15ae6150 ("Part of #869"). Build, clippy with -D warnings, fmt --check, the full cargo test (every test binary, 0 failed) and tests/harness_logic.sh (182 passed) are all green.

How it behaves
• A path must pass both the startup fence and the new directory's allow list, so /cd can only narrow. Relative entries like src are tied to the directory you /cd'd into, the same way deny entries already were.
• Each `/cd` **replaces** the allow list; deny entries still only accumulate. Two unrelated projects' allow lists would together refuse almost everything. A replacement can only widen back to the launch fence, never past it. The reason is written in the doc comment on apply_cd_dir_fence_with.
• **Placement:** the check sits inside `cd_denied_dir_refusal_with`, the function every `/cd`-aware reader already calls. So the file tools, `@path`/`/add`/`/explain/--image, /goal check, auto-context and /rename all got it without new wiring. The list of readers that skip the /cd fence (UNWIRED_CHECK_PATH_READERS`) is unchanged: 2 rows, both startup-only.
• The list itself is stored separately (CD_ALLOWED_DIRS), next to the deny list. Storing both in one shared value would have changed every reader's signature. The cost: a reader given a test-only deny list still reads the real allow list, which is empty in tests.

Beyond the planned 3 files. I didn't stop at the 3-file limit, because none of these is a parallel wrapper:
• `dispatch.rs: one call in the /cd` handler.
• `commands_config.rs: one argument so /permissions` gets the allow list.
• `commands_rename.rs: its shortcut only checked whether the /cd deny list was empty, so a /cd` that set only an allow list would have skipped the fence. I found it by grepping for that emptiness test, not by listing who calls the check.
• **Renamed label:** `CD_DENY_SOURCE_NOTE` became `CD_DIR_SOURCE_NOTE`, now reading `(a [directories] entry applied by /cd)`. It is appended to every `/cd` refusal, so an allow refusal would have been labelled "deny entry", which is false. Three tests that quote the text were updated.

Visible to the user
• `/permissions lists each /cd allow entry under Allowed directories`, labelled "(an allow entry applied by /cd; also required, replaced by the next /cd)".
• The `/cd` warning reports the allow list as applied rather than unapplied.

Tests: I added four in cd_config_note.rs:
• real files admitted or refused, with the exact refusal text;
• a directory with no allow list gives byte-identical refusals to before;
• the startup allow list still applies alongside a /cd allow list;
• the next /cd replaces the allow list and keeps the deny entries.

I also added an exact-line test for /permissions. For the positive control I disabled the allow check (marked NEUTERED) in one command that also restored it. Exactly the three allow tests failed by name and the near-miss test stayed green. After the restore everything passed and no NEUTERED marker is left.

